"With rising traffic congestion and attempt towards minimizing the carbon footprint of vehicles, trams, and public transport, the city administration have introduced CityBike service where riders\ncan opt to use bikes available via pay-per-use renting for enhanced mobility and eco-friendly transportation in and around the city. City administration needs an software application CityBike,\navailable on web and mobile devices that can offer the potential bikers to utilize the bikes.\n\nThe system must take into account the following constraints and EU standards:\n- The software should comply with the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) and the Privacy Directive.\n- The software should comply with the relevant security and data protection standards, such as ISO 27001.\n- The software should be designed to minimize the carbon footprint and promote sustainable mobility.\n- The software should be user-friendly and easy to use for all potential riders.\n- The software should be designed to ensure the security and privacy of user data, including payment information.\n- The software should be compatible with a variety of third-party payment platforms to allow users to pay for the bike rental fee\nseamlessly.\n- The software should be capable of tracking and displaying the location of available bikes within a 1000-meter radius and unlocking\nthem using a QR code.\n- The software should be accessible through both web and mobile devices to maximize the availability of the service.\n- The software should be designed to comply with local regulations and standards, including EU or China standards, depending on the\nlocation of the university. \n\nThe design of the system can rely on the following tools and technologies: container orchestration platform, service registry and discovery, API gateway, message broker, and distributed tracing."
