{
  "system_name": "campus-bike",
  "analysis_notes": [
    "Pattern names are copied exactly from pattern_candidates.json; affected_drivers are copied exactly from drivers.json.",
    "Relationship vocabulary: 'compatible' (can coexist without special effort), 'complementary' (each strengthens the other for a driver), 'conflict' (pull in opposite directions; may be combinable under conditions), 'mutually_exclusive' (alternative answers to the same structural question), 'redundant' (overlapping responsibility; using both adds cost without benefit unless conditions hold), 'dependency' (first pattern effectively requires the second to address the drivers).",
    "The only pattern not in the candidate list mentioned here is the Transactional Outbox, cited as a condition (not a candidate) where events must be published reliably after an atomic state change; it is introduced because S7 requires both an atomic availability change and its propagation.",
    "No complete alternatives are formed here; that is Task 3."
  ],
  "compatibility_analysis": [
    {
      "patterns": ["Modular Monolith (domain-partitioned modules in a single deployable)", "Microservices"],
      "relationship": "mutually_exclusive",
      "rationale": "Both answer the same question: how the backend is decomposed into deployable units. A design is primarily one or the other. They trade off differently on the drivers: the monolith makes atomic bike/rental consistency (S6/S7) simple and minimizes runtime components (carbon footprint), while microservices give fault isolation for rider availability and isolate sensitive-data and integration-heavy capabilities.",
      "conditions": [
        "A hybrid is possible: a modular monolith core (bike availability, rentals) with a small number of extracted services for capabilities whose isolation is evidenced (e.g. payment integration, notifications, device gateway). Such a hybrid must state which boundaries are extracted and why.",
